    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved all date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starts **1 July**, named for the calendar year it ends in \u2014 e.g. `2026-09-07` \u2192 **FY27**). All computations use the already business-day-adjusted `registrationDate` (rolled forward in `BuildOwner`).\n\n**New file**\n- `rest/function/owner/FiscalYear.java` \u2014 single source of truth: `of(date)` returns the four-digit fiscal year (`month >= 7 ? year+1 : year`) and `label(date)` returns `FY<YY>`.\n\n**Changed**\n- `mapper/OwnerMapper.java` \u2014 added the new `fiscalYear` mapping (`FiscalYear.label(...)`), and switched the `membershipNumber` year segment from `registrationDate.getYear() % 100` to `FiscalYear.of(registrationDate) % 100`.\n- `rest/function/owner/MembershipLevel.java` \u2014 tenure now counts **elapsed fiscal years** (`FiscalYear.of(now) - FiscalYear.of(registrationDate)`) rather than a 365-day window; the tenure points are awarded when more than one fiscal year has elapsed.\n- `rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java` \u2014 audit-log membership number kept in sync with the fiscal-year formula.\n- `resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` (`FY<YY>`) property to the `Owner` schema and refreshed the now-stale `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions.\n\nThe new `coreReturnsFiscalYear` acceptance test passes: `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns `fiscalYear` matching `FY\\d{2}`, and the audit log confirms the membership number now uses the fiscal year (`...-M27` for a 2026-09-07 registration).\n\nNote on the one deliberate interpretive choice, in case you want it decided differently: I labelled the fiscal year by the calendar year it **ends** in (Australian convention, fitting the app's NSW/VIC/QLD data), and set the tenure threshold to \"more than one elapsed fiscal year\" to preserve the prior \"over one year\" semantics. Neither is pinned by a test, so both are easy to flip if your spec intends otherwise.",
